---
// Privacy policy, matched to what's actually operating (28 Oct 2026):
// enquiries arrive by email (Gmail). Facebook and Instagram adverts link to
// this website. Meta instant lead forms have never been used to collect
// anyone's details, so Meta isn't a provider for enquiry data. The site is
// hosted on GitHub Pages with
// self-hosted fonts and sets no cookies or web storage, and has no Meta
// Pixel, analytics or other tracking (checked in the built site and in a
// browser, 28 Oct 2026). There is no website form, no
// payment provider, no CRM and no spreadsheet service in use. Weekly
// backups of hosted client websites' repositories go to Backblaze B2, EU
// Central (Amsterdam), under Backblaze's UK DPA (since 23 Oct 2026). The
// earlier Dropbox archives were deleted on 23 Oct 2026; Dropbox Basic keeps
// deleted files recoverable for 30 days, so mention Dropbox until 22 Nov
// 2026, then remove it from this page.
// Client websites currently hosted by Matt are on GitHub
// Pages (verified 22 Oct 2026 for barberwarnz.co.uk and
// leroydrivinginstructor.co.uk). If any of that changes, update this page
// first.
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { contact, business } from "../data/config";

const lastUpdated = "28 October 2026";

const sections = [
  { id: "who", label: "Who I am" },
  { id: "collect", label: "What I collect" },
  { id: "use", label: "How I use it" },
  { id: "marketing", label: "Marketing" },
  { id: "providers", label: "Who handles it" },
  { id: "transfers", label: "Outside the UK" },
  { id: "retention", label: "How long I keep it" },
  { id: "cookies", label: "Cookies and tracking" },
  { id: "rights", label: "Your rights" },
  { id: "complaints", label: "Complaints" },
  { id: "changes", label: "Changes" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy | Stapled."
  description="How Stapled. uses the details you send by email, how long they're kept, and your rights. This website uses no cookies or tracking."
  path="/privacy/"
  heading="Privacy policy"
  intro="This explains what I do with the details you send me when you ask about a website, and what you can ask me to do about it."
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  sections={sections}
>
    <section id="who">
      <h2>Who I am</h2>
      <p>
        Stapled. is run by {business.proprietor}, a sole trader based in
        Southampton, UK, trading as {business.tradingAs} I'm the person
        responsible for your information (the "controller" under UK data
        protection law).
      </p>
      <p>
        For anything to do with your information, email me at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a>, or write to
        the {business.addressLabel.toLowerCase()} on the{" "}
        <a href="/business-details/">business details</a> page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="collect">
      <h2>What I collect</h2>
      <p>
        I only collect what you choose to send me.
      </p>

      <h3>Emails you send me</h3>
      <p>
        You can ask about a website or any of my services by email. I'll
        have whatever you include, usually your name, email address,
        business name, what the business does and where, a link to your
        current website or social media page, and what you'd like help
        with. A phone
        number is only needed if you'd like me to call you.
      </p>

      <h3>Adverts on Facebook and Instagram</h3>
      <p>
        My adverts on Facebook and Instagram link to this website. Clicking
        one doesn't send me any details about you: you only reach me if you
        then get in touch by email. Meta, which runs Facebook and Instagram,
        uses information about your activity on its platforms, including how
        you interact with adverts, under its own privacy policy. This
        website doesn't use Meta's tracking pixel, so Meta doesn't receive
        information about what you do here.
      </p>

      <h3>If you become a client</h3>
      <p>
        I'll also have your quote and acceptance, our correspondence, the
        content you give me for your website, and records of your payments.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="use">
      <h2>How I use it, and why I'm allowed to</h2>
      <p>
        UK data protection law requires a "lawful basis" for each use of
        your information. Here's what I do and the basis I rely on.
      </p>

      <dl class="pp-uses">
        <div class="pp-use">
          <dt>Replying to your enquiry and discussing your project</dt>
          <dd>
            <span class="pp-use__basis">Legitimate interests</span>{" "}
            You've asked me to get in touch, and I have a legitimate
            interest in replying and talking to you about the work. If
            you're asking as an individual about buying a website, this is
            also a step you've asked me to take before we enter into a
            contract.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pp-use">
          <dt>Preparing a homepage preview, if I've offered one</dt>
          <dd>
            <span class="pp-use__basis">Legitimate interests</span>{" "}
            For some enquiries I offer a quick homepage preview. If you'd
            like one, I use your business details, and any website or
            social link you give me, to put it together.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pp-use">
          <dt>Building and looking after your website, if you become a client</dt>
          <dd>
            <span class="pp-use__basis">Contract</span>{" "}
            I use your details to do the work we've agreed and to take
            payment for it.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pp-use">
          <dt>Keeping business and tax records</dt>
          <dd>
            <span class="pp-use__basis">Legal obligation</span>{" "}
            I keep records of payments and invoices because the law requires
            it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p>
        You don't have to give me any of this information, and there's no
        legal requirement to. Without a way to contact you, though, I can't
        reply. I don't make any decisions about you by automated means.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="marketing">
      <h2>Marketing</h2>
      <p>
        I only use enquiry details to reply to you, discuss your project
        and, if I've offered one and you'd like it, prepare a homepage
        preview. You don't need to
        agree to marketing to make an enquiry. I won't add you to a
        newsletter or promotional campaign, and I won't upload your details
        to Meta or anyone else to target you with adverts.
      </p>
      <p>
        If that ever changes, I'll ask for your agreement separately first,
        and you'll be able to change your mind at any time, free of charge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="providers">
      <h2>Who handles your information</h2>
      <p>
        I don't sell your information or share it with anyone for their own
        marketing. These services handle it on the way through:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong> provides the Gmail account where your
          emails arrive and where I keep our conversations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>GitHub</strong> hosts this website (GitHub Pages). Like any
          web host, it processes technical information such as your IP
          address so it can deliver the pages to you. The client websites I
          currently host are also on GitHub Pages, so if you're a client I
          host, your website's files and the content you've given me for it
          are stored with GitHub too.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Backblaze</strong> stores the weekly backups of the client
          websites I host, in its data centre in Amsterdam. So if you're a
          client I host, copies of your website's code and files are stored
          with Backblaze too.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dropbox</strong> briefly held a few earlier backup copies of
          those websites in October 2026, before I moved backups to
          Backblaze. I deleted them on 23 October 2026. Dropbox keeps deleted
          files recoverable for 30 days before removing them permanently, so
          they'll be gone from Dropbox by 22 November 2026. No new backups go
          to Dropbox.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>My bank</strong> processes any payments you make to me by
          bank transfer.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I don't currently use a payment provider, customer database or
        spreadsheet service for your details. If I start using one, or host
        a client's website somewhere other than GitHub, I'll update this
        policy first.
      </p>
      <p>
        I may also share information if the law requires me to, for example
        with HMRC or in response to a valid legal request.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="transfers">
      <h2>Information sent outside the UK</h2>
      <p>
        Backblaze stores backups in the European Economic Area, which UK law
        recognises as providing adequate protection. Google, GitHub and
        Dropbox are based in, or store information in, the
        United States and other countries outside the UK. They each describe
        in their own privacy information the safeguards they rely on for
        this, such as UK adequacy regulations (including the UK–US "data
        bridge") or approved contract terms. Email me if you'd like more
        detail.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="retention">
      <h2>How long I keep it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Enquiries that don't lead to work:</strong> up to 12 months
          after we were last in meaningful contact. Then I delete them from
          my email, unless there's a recorded reason to keep
          one longer, such as an unresolved complaint or you asking me to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Client records</strong> (your quote and acceptance, our
          correspondence and project files): 6 years after our work together
          ends, which is the usual time limit for claims about a contract.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your website's files</strong> (if you were a client): once
          hosting has ended and the handover is complete, I keep a recovery
          copy for 30 days, then delete it. Backup copies are removed
          automatically after about 8 weeks (weekly copies) or about 6 months
          (monthly copies), and permanently deleted within a further 30 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accounting records</strong> (payments and invoices): as
          long as HMRC requires. For a sole trader, that's at least 5 years
          after the 31 January Self Assessment deadline for the tax year
          they relate to.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="cookies">
      <h2>Cookies and tracking</h2>
      <p>
        This website doesn't set cookies, doesn't store anything in your
        browser, and doesn't use analytics, advertising pixels or other
        tracking tools. Everything on its pages, fonts included, is served
        from this site's own hosting. That's why there's no
        cookie banner: there's nothing to agree to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Email links open your own email app. This site also links to other
        websites, such as Instagram. Once you follow a
        link, that site's own privacy policy applies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="rights">
      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>You have the right to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>ask for a copy of the information I hold about you</li>
        <li>ask me to correct anything that's wrong or incomplete</li>
        <li>ask me to delete your information</li>
        <li>ask me to limit how I use it</li>
        <li>
          ask me to send information you gave me to you or another
          organisation, where that applies
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="pp-callout">
        <h3>Your right to object</h3>
        <p>
          Because I rely on legitimate interests, you can object to me
          using your information at any time. You can always object to
          marketing, and I'll stop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        To use any of these rights, email{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a>. It's
        free, and I'll reply within one month. I may need to confirm who
        you are first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="complaints">
      <h2>Complaints</h2>
      <p>
        If you're unhappy with how I've handled your information, please
        email me at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a> first. I'll
        acknowledge your complaint within 30 days, look into it, and let
        you know the outcome without undue delay.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also have the right to complain to the Information
        Commissioner's Office (ICO), the UK's data protection
        regulator, at{" "}
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>{" "}
        or on 0303 123 1113. The ICO will usually ask you to raise it
        with me first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changes">
      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If I change how I handle your information, I'll update this page
        and the date at the top.
      </p>
    </section>
</LegalLayout>
